<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stefan Uroš I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1223–<a href="/years/1277">1277</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemanjic-dynasty">Nemanjić dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stefan Uroš I was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> from 1243 to 1276. He built up the kingdom's economy by inviting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> miners, who opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brskovo">Brskovo</a>, and by encouraging trade with Ragusa. He founded the monastery of Sopoćani, and in 1276 he was deposed by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dragutin">Stefan Dragutin</a> and ended his life as a monk.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-i.jpg" alt="Stefan Uroš I" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: le_serbe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Stefan_Uro%C5%A1_I_with_his_son_Stefan_Dragutin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stefan Uroš was the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-the-first-crowned">Stefan the First-Crowned</a> and Anna, a granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enrico-dandolo">Enrico Dandolo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doge-of-venice">Doge of Venice</a>. He had two older half-brothers, Stefan Radoslav and Stefan Vladislav, who both reigned before him. Scholars have argued that strong and unpopular Bulgarian influence caused the opposition that led to Vladislav's deposition. The rebellious nobility chose Uroš as their candidate, and from 1242 to the spring of 1243 a war for the throne ended with Vladislav forced to give up the crown. Vladislav's wife, Beloslava, led the main resistance. Uroš treated his brother courteously and gave him the administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Uroš took the throne at about 25, and the land had been looted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> and torn by internal conflicts. He pushed south into Macedonia and came into conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. He subordinated both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and the Catholic churches to the interests of the state and helped settle a dispute between the archbishoprics of Bar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> in favour of Bar. He protected writers and encouraged a new and fuller biography of his grandfather Nemanja, on whom he modelled himself, and he lived modestly at a court where everyone worked.</p><p>He was the first to exploit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>'s mines, which became a main source of its medieval wealth. They were developed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, who lived by their own laws in settlements near the mines and could keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and build their churches, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novo-brdo">Novo Brdo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brskovo">Brskovo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudnik">Rudnik</a>. Trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> and Kotor grew, and Serbian coins were minted on the Venetian model.</p><p>He fought Ragusa in 1252–1253, ending the war with a charter of 22 May 1254, and again in the later 1260s, when a treaty of 1268 fixed the tribute <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> paid the Serbian crown each year. In 1268 he invaded Hungarian lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macva">Mačva</a>, was captured and had to buy his release. The peace married his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dragutin">Stefan Dragutin</a> to Catherine, daughter of the future King Stephen V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Uroš also allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a> against Emperor Michael Palaiologos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Uroš refused his eldest son an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> and appears to have considered making his younger son, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-milutin">Stefan Milutin</a>, his heir. In 1276 Dragutin demanded the throne, and when his father refused he rebelled with Hungarian help and defeated him. Uroš abdicated and retired to a monastery in Hum, where he died soon afterwards. His remains were later moved to his foundation at Sopoćani.</p><p>His reign laid the foundations for the expansion of the Serbian medieval state, which became an empire in the middle of the 14th century. He is known as Uroš the Great and is counted among the most important rulers in Serbian history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stefan Uroš I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Uroš I</b> invited Saxon miners, opening the silver mine at Brskovo; later mines such as Novo Brdo made Serbia rich and supplied Ragusa's trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-serbia-medieval">Kingdom of Serbia (medieval)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sopocani-monastery">Sopoćani monastery</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.12,20.42&amp;year=1258" aria-label="Sopoćani monastery on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5043 633 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Stefan Uroš I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5493 903h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5493 903h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=43.12,20.42&amp;year=1258">What was here in 1258? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
